 ## Background
-The concept of progressive training in radiance fields originates from the need to stabilize the optimization of high-dimensional, non-convex loss landscapes [S1, unofficial]. Early 3D Gaussian Splatting implementations used a single-stage optimization with fixed densification schedules [S1, unofficial]. However, researchers observed that initializing with a sparse set of primitives or using curriculum learning (e.g., starting with low-resolution or simplified supervision) led to better convergence and higher final fidelity [S1, unofficial][S2]. The Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy and MeshGS variants emerged to address specific geometric constraints, while curriculum learning for volumetric data addressed the unique challenges of 3D medical or scientific imaging [S1, unofficial][S2]. The Two-Stage Training Scheme for FWD-U highlights the broader applicability of staged optimization to depth estimation in multi-view stereo tasks [S3].
+The concept of multi-stage or coarse-to-fine training in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a response to the instability and local minima issues inherent in optimizing a large number of primitives from random or sparse initializations. Early works like the original 3DGS [22] used a single-stage optimization with periodic densification, which could lead to artifacts if the initial point cloud was insufficient. Subsequent research introduced hybrid strategies, such as the Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy, which uses mesh-based initialization and Delaunay triangulation to ensure better initial coverage [S2]. Other approaches, like MeshGS, introduced stage-specific loss functions and densification rules to align Gaussians with explicit geometry [S1, unofficial]. The Fine-tuning approach leverages feed-forward networks to provide high-quality initializations, reducing the need for extensive densification [S1, unofficial]. Additionally, curriculum learning techniques, such as Slice and Voxel Supervision, have been proposed to stabilize the optimization of volumetric data by alternating between local and global constraints [S1, unofficial]. The Two-Stage Training Scheme for FWD-U highlights the importance of initializing depth networks separately to avoid instability in multi-view synthesis tasks [S3]. These methods collectively demonstrate that decoupling initialization, densification, and optimization stages leads to more robust and higher-quality 3D reconstructions.
 
